data: {"invocationId":"e-472eff97-dd30-44e7-a678-50422083d0a1","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{"intake":{"question":"Should I open a second location for my bakery, or invest in delivery first?","leaning":"Open the second location. A storefront just came up for lease on Route 1.","profile":{"name":"Rise & Shine Bakery","industry":"neighborhood bakery and cafe","location":"College Park, Maryland","size":"1 location, 7 employees","current_tools":["Square POS","Instagram","Google Business Profile"],"budget":"$90,000 in savings","goals":["grow revenue","don't put the first store at risk"],"constraints":"Revenue is about $42,000 a month. Savings cover six months of runway. I bake every morning myself and have no manager yet."}},"evidence_mode":"web"},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":"Assemble the shared evidence for this decision: Should I open a second location for my bakery, or invest in delivery first? (neighborhood bakery and cafe, College Park, Maryland)","nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/intake@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/intake@1"]},"id":"01ee1ebe-3e3b-4bae-b5b1-0f9eea261fe1","timestamp":1794351725.9430928}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"I'm going to run several searches at once: Square's pricing, DoorDash's commission fees, the Maryland license rules for food service in Prince George's County, the 2026 minimum wage, the cost of a second bakery location, and retail rent on Route 1 in College Park.\n\nI still need a few more inputs: bakery profit margin benchmarks, delivery app fees, College Park retail rent along Route 1, and Maryland sales tax. Square's pricing didn't come back from the vendor page, so I'll search squareup.com's US pricing directly, alongside the margin benchmark, Route 1 rent, and the University of Maryland calendar and summer slump.\n\nNow I'm pulling together the pricing facts for each platform: Square's plans at $0, $49, and $149, DoorDash's merchant pricing page, and Uber Eats' merchant terms. I also need to cover Square Online and the Maryland and Prince George's County health permit fees of $449 to $845 plus a $66 admin charge.\n\nI'm checking the Prince George's County permit fees now, $449 to $845 plus a $66 administrative charge, and I'm noting that the figures come from an older schedule that's been superseded. I'm also looking at a Route 1 rental listing at $25 per square foot per year, though it may not be the owner's own space.\n\nI'm settling on the facts to include: a second location runs about $75K to $200K, and second-generation build-outs can save 40 to 60 percent. I'm also noting that a three-phase power retrofit can add $10K to $40K, and that a Route 1 listing at $25 per square foot works out to roughly $2,970 a month before utilities, though it may not be the owner's own space.\n\nNow I'm weighing how to split the pricing facts so each product gets one clear line: Square's plans with their monthly prices, including whether the $49 tier is charged per location, which matters for a second site. I'm also considering whether to fold in Square Online and the courier fees that Square's delivery relies on.\n\nNext I'm listing the pricing and cost figures I'd want to cite, starting with Square's $49 plan and whether that charge repeats at each location. Then I'm lining up DoorDash and Uber Eats' commission tiers, the Route 1 rent and second-location build-out ranges, and the Prince George's County wage and permit numbers.\n\nI'm settling on the Square plan details now: the $49 tier, the $0 free plan, and the $149 premium option, with a note on whether the monthly charge applies per location. For Square's online orders I'm checking the commission-free ordering rate and the third-party courier fees that delivery depends on.\n\nI'm settling on the options to present: doing nothing, starting with delivery through my own online ordering alongside a third-party test, opening a second location, or a hybrid that hires a manager first, pilots delivery, and delays the lease while negotiating.\n\n","thought":true},{"text":"{\"decision\":\"Should Rise & Shine Bakery (College Park, MD) sign a lease and open a second location, or invest in delivery first, or neither?\",\"options\":[\"Do nothing for now: keep one location, build cash, and revisit in 6 to 12 months\",\"Delivery and online ordering first: use the existing Square online page plus a limited marketplace test (DoorDash Basic or Uber Eats Lite) or a small in-house delivery radius\",\"Open the second location on Route 1 now, using savings and possibly financing\",\"Sequence it: hire or train a manager and test delivery for 90 days, then negotiate the Route 1 lease or look at other spaces\"],\"facts\":[{\"claim\":\"A second retail bakery location is commonly estimated at $75K to $200K, and bakery startup costs overall at $50K to $250K. The lower end of that range is close to the owner's $90,000 in savings. Estimates vary widely by source and by space condition.\",\"source\":\"https://primerates.com/business-loans/bakery-business-loans/\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Another industry guide puts a retail bakery in a second-generation food space at $120K to $350K, and a raw-shell bakery-cafe at $350K to $700K or more. It says deck ovens often need three-phase power, and retrofitting a space without it can cost $10K to $40K.\",\"source\":\"https://www.tableview.com/blog/how-to-open-a-bakery/\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"A space that already has a hood, grease trap and three-compartment sink can cut build-out cost by about 40% to 60%. Whether the Route 1 storefront has these is unknown and should be checked before signing.\",\"source\":\"https://startupcosthub.com/costs/bakery-startup-costs\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"A LoopNet listing for a 1,425 SF retail suite on Route 1 (Baltimore Ave) in College Park asks $25/SF/yr plus all utilities. That is roughly $35,600 a year, or about $2,970 a month, in base rent. The College Park retail average on LoopNet is about $36/SF. This is a comparable listing, not necessarily the owner's storefront.\",\"source\":\"https://www.loopnet.com/Listing/7236-7370-Baltimore-Ave-College-Park-MD/17610853/\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Bakery benchmarks: food cost of 28% to 35% of revenue, labor of 25% to 35% including owner pay, and net profit of 5% to 15%. Other sources put the average retail bakery at 4% to 9% net. At $42K a month, a 5% to 15% margin is roughly $2,100 to $6,300 a month before owner pay.\",\"source\":\"https://www.bakeonyx.ai/resources/bakery-benchmarks\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"The Prince George's County minimum wage is $15.30/hr for all employers, above the $15.00 state rate. College Park is in Prince George's County, so a second location in the county would pay the same rate. A location in a neighboring county such as Montgomery or Howard would face different, higher local wage rates.\",\"source\":\"https://fingercheck.com/payroll-software/maryland-minimum-wage/\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Prince George's County requires a food service facility permit from the Health Department and plan review through the permitting agency. A certified food service manager must be on duty during all hours of operation. First-time certification is a 16-hour course plus an exam, with a $35 county fee. A second storefront would need its own permit and certified manager coverage, which matters because the owner has no manager.\",\"source\":\"https://www.dcfoodhandlers.com/local-requirements/prince-georges-county-health-department.html\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"A Prince George's County fee schedule lists food service facility permit fees of $449 (low HACCP priority), $660 (moderate) and $845 (high), each tied to a $66 administrative fee. The schedule is older and a March 2025 revision exists, so confirm current fees with the county. Operating without a certified manager carries a listed fee of about $600 or more.\",\"source\":\"https://www.princegeorgescountymd.gov/sites/default/files/media-document/DPIE%20Fee%20Schedule.pdf\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"DoorDash Marketplace charges no monthly or signup fee. Delivery commission is 15% on Basic, 25% on Plus and 30% on Premier, and pickup is 6%. Free trials are 7 days at 0% on Basic and 30 days on Plus and Premier.\",\"source\":\"https://merchants.doordash.com/en-us/pricing\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Uber Eats Marketplace delivery commission is 20% on Lite, 25% on Plus (30% on Uber One member orders) and 30% on Premium. Lite was raised from 15% in March 2026. Pickup is 7% with validated in-store pricing.\",\"source\":\"https://www.uber.com/us/en/legal/uber-eats-merchant-terms-and-conditions/\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Square for Restaurants (the owner's current POS) is $0/mo on Square Free, $49/mo per location on Square Plus and $149/mo per location on Square Premium. A KDS screen is $30/mo per device on Plus and $20 on Premium. Plans are per location, so a second store adds its own subscription. There are no contracts.\",\"source\":\"https://squareup.com/us/en/campaigns/discover-restaurants\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Square's own online ordering page (pickup and local delivery) is included on every plan with no marketplace-style commission. Card processing online is 3.3% + 30¢ on Free and 2.9% + 30¢ on Plus and Premium. In-person card processing is 2.6% + 15¢ on Free, 2.5% + 15¢ on Plus and 2.4% + 15¢ on Premium.\",\"source\":\"https://www.therestauranthq.com/technology/square-for-restaurants-review/\",\"verified\":true},{\"claim\":\"Square Online delivery can use a third-party courier such as DoorDash or Uber, which charges per delivery. One report cites about $6.99 and up per delivery, and Square's own per-delivery fee was dropped in February 2025. This is a secondary source, so confirm on Square's delivery pricing page.\",\"source\":\"https://www.adamssnackwagon.com/square-online-ordering-for-restaurants/\",\"verified\":true}],\"open_questions\":[\"What are the Route 1 space's square footage, rent type (NNN or gross), lease term, personal guarantee, rent-free build-out period and landlord tenant-improvement allowance? Is it a former food space with a hood, grease trap and three-phase power?\",\"What do the first store's actual monthly profit, owner pay and cash cushion look like? $42K a month in revenue does not show what is left after the owner's own pay.\",\"How seasonal is the first store's revenue with the University of Maryland calendar (summer, winter break)? Would a Route 1 location face the same swings?\",\"Who runs the first store's morning bake if the owner is at the second location? What would a qualified manager or head baker cost, and is a certified food service manager already on staff?\",\"What share of current sales might delivery or online ordering add? How much do customers already ask for delivery, and can baked goods hold up in transit?\",\"Is the second location meant to be a full bakery or a retail satellite fed from the existing kitchen? Does the current kitchen have spare oven capacity?\",\"Could financing (such as an SBA loan) or a landlord allowance cover build-out, so savings stay as a reserve for the first store?\",\"What would signing the lease cost in total (deposit, personal guarantee, 5-year obligation) if the second store underperforms?\",\"What are the College Park and Prince George's County zoning, signage and use-and-occupancy requirements for this specific storefront, and how long would approvals take?\"]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":5226,"promptTokenCount":142665,"thoughtsTokenCount":1985,"totalTokenCount":147891},"invocationId":"e-472eff97-dd30-44e7-a678-50422083d0a1","author":"researcher","actions":{"stateDelta":{"evidence":{"decision":"Should Rise & Shine Bakery (College Park, MD) sign a lease and open a second location, or invest in delivery first, or neither?","options":["Do nothing for now: keep one location, build cash, and revisit in 6 to 12 months","Delivery and online ordering first: use the existing Square online page plus a limited marketplace test (DoorDash Basic or Uber Eats Lite) or a small in-house delivery radius","Open the second location on Route 1 now, using savings and possibly financing","Sequence it: hire or train a manager and test delivery for 90 days, then negotiate the Route 1 lease or look at other spaces"],"facts":[{"claim":"A second retail bakery location is commonly estimated at $75K to $200K, and bakery startup costs overall at $50K to $250K. The lower end of that range is close to the owner's $90,000 in savings. Estimates vary widely by source and by space condition.","source":"https://primerates.com/business-loans/bakery-business-loans/","verified":true},{"claim":"Another industry guide puts a retail bakery in a second-generation food space at $120K to $350K, and a raw-shell bakery-cafe at $350K to $700K or more. It says deck ovens often need three-phase power, and retrofitting a space without it can cost $10K to $40K.","source":"https://www.tableview.com/blog/how-to-open-a-bakery/","verified":true},{"claim":"A space that already has a hood, grease trap and three-compartment sink can cut build-out cost by about 40% to 60%. Whether the Route 1 storefront has these is unknown and should be checked before signing.","source":"https://startupcosthub.com/costs/bakery-startup-costs","verified":true},{"claim":"A LoopNet listing for a 1,425 SF retail suite on Route 1 (Baltimore Ave) in College Park asks $25/SF/yr plus all utilities. That is roughly $35,600 a year, or about $2,970 a month, in base rent. The College Park retail average on LoopNet is about $36/SF. This is a comparable listing, not necessarily the owner's storefront.","source":"https://www.loopnet.com/Listing/7236-7370-Baltimore-Ave-College-Park-MD/17610853/","verified":true},{"claim":"Bakery benchmarks: food cost of 28% to 35% of revenue, labor of 25% to 35% including owner pay, and net profit of 5% to 15%. Other sources put the average retail bakery at 4% to 9% net. At $42K a month, a 5% to 15% margin is roughly $2,100 to $6,300 a month before owner pay.","source":"https://www.bakeonyx.ai/resources/bakery-benchmarks","verified":true},{"claim":"The Prince George's County minimum wage is $15.30/hr for all employers, above the $15.00 state rate. College Park is in Prince George's County, so a second location in the county would pay the same rate. A location in a neighboring county such as Montgomery or Howard would face different, higher local wage rates.","source":"https://fingercheck.com/payroll-software/maryland-minimum-wage/","verified":true},{"claim":"Prince George's County requires a food service facility permit from the Health Department and plan review through the permitting agency. A certified food service manager must be on duty during all hours of operation. First-time certification is a 16-hour course plus an exam, with a $35 county fee. A second storefront would need its own permit and certified manager coverage, which matters because the owner has no manager.","source":"https://www.dcfoodhandlers.com/local-requirements/prince-georges-county-health-department.html","verified":true},{"claim":"A Prince George's County fee schedule lists food service facility permit fees of $449 (low HACCP priority), $660 (moderate) and $845 (high), each tied to a $66 administrative fee. The schedule is older and a March 2025 revision exists, so confirm current fees with the county. Operating without a certified manager carries a listed fee of about $600 or more.","source":"https://www.princegeorgescountymd.gov/sites/default/files/media-document/DPIE%20Fee%20Schedule.pdf","verified":true},{"claim":"DoorDash Marketplace charges no monthly or signup fee. Delivery commission is 15% on Basic, 25% on Plus and 30% on Premier, and pickup is 6%. Free trials are 7 days at 0% on Basic and 30 days on Plus and Premier.","source":"https://merchants.doordash.com/en-us/pricing","verified":true},{"claim":"Uber Eats Marketplace delivery commission is 20% on Lite, 25% on Plus (30% on Uber One member orders) and 30% on Premium. Lite was raised from 15% in March 2026. Pickup is 7% with validated in-store pricing.","source":"https://www.uber.com/us/en/legal/uber-eats-merchant-terms-and-conditions/","verified":true},{"claim":"Square for Restaurants (the owner's current POS) is $0/mo on Square Free, $49/mo per location on Square Plus and $149/mo per location on Square Premium. A KDS screen is $30/mo per device on Plus and $20 on Premium. Plans are per location, so a second store adds its own subscription. There are no contracts.","source":"https://squareup.com/us/en/campaigns/discover-restaurants","verified":true},{"claim":"Square's own online ordering page (pickup and local delivery) is included on every plan with no marketplace-style commission. Card processing online is 3.3% + 30¢ on Free and 2.9% + 30¢ on Plus and Premium. In-person card processing is 2.6% + 15¢ on Free, 2.5% + 15¢ on Plus and 2.4% + 15¢ on Premium.","source":"https://www.therestauranthq.com/technology/square-for-restaurants-review/","verified":true},{"claim":"Square Online delivery can use a third-party courier such as DoorDash or Uber, which charges per delivery. One report cites about $6.99 and up per delivery, and Square's own per-delivery fee was dropped in February 2025. This is a secondary source, so confirm on Square's delivery pricing page.","source":"https://www.adamssnackwagon.com/square-online-ordering-for-restaurants/","verified":true}],"open_questions":["What are the Route 1 space's square footage, rent type (NNN or gross), lease term, personal guarantee, rent-free build-out period and landlord tenant-improvement allowance? Is it a former food space with a hood, grease trap and three-phase power?","What do the first store's actual monthly profit, owner pay and cash cushion look like? $42K a month in revenue does not show what is left after the owner's own pay.","How seasonal is the first store's revenue with the University of Maryland calendar (summer, winter break)? Would a Route 1 location face the same swings?","Who runs the first store's morning bake if the owner is at the second location? What would a qualified manager or head baker cost, and is a certified food service manager already on staff?","What share of current sales might delivery or online ordering add? How much do customers already ask for delivery, and can baked goods hold up in transit?","Is the second location meant to be a full bakery or a retail satellite fed from the existing kitchen? Does the current kitchen have spare oven capacity?","Could financing (such as an SBA loan) or a landlord allowance cover build-out, so savings stay as a reserve for the first store?","What would signing the lease cost in total (deposit, personal guarantee, 5-year obligation) if the second store underperforms?","What are the College Park and Prince George's County zoning, signage and use-and-occupancy requirements for this specific storefront, and how long would approvals take?"]}},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"decision":"Should Rise & Shine Bakery (College Park, MD) sign a lease and open a second location, or invest in delivery first, or neither?","options":["Do nothing for now: keep one location, build cash, and revisit in 6 to 12 months","Delivery and online ordering first: use the existing Square online page plus a limited marketplace test (DoorDash Basic or Uber Eats Lite) or a small in-house delivery radius","Open the second location on Route 1 now, using savings and possibly financing","Sequence it: hire or train a manager and test delivery for 90 days, then negotiate the Route 1 lease or look at other spaces"],"facts":[{"claim":"A second retail bakery location is commonly estimated at $75K to $200K, and bakery startup costs overall at $50K to $250K. The lower end of that range is close to the owner's $90,000 in savings. Estimates vary widely by source and by space condition.","source":"https://primerates.com/business-loans/bakery-business-loans/","verified":true},{"claim":"Another industry guide puts a retail bakery in a second-generation food space at $120K to $350K, and a raw-shell bakery-cafe at $350K to $700K or more. It says deck ovens often need three-phase power, and retrofitting a space without it can cost $10K to $40K.","source":"https://www.tableview.com/blog/how-to-open-a-bakery/","verified":true},{"claim":"A space that already has a hood, grease trap and three-compartment sink can cut build-out cost by about 40% to 60%. Whether the Route 1 storefront has these is unknown and should be checked before signing.","source":"https://startupcosthub.com/costs/bakery-startup-costs","verified":true},{"claim":"A LoopNet listing for a 1,425 SF retail suite on Route 1 (Baltimore Ave) in College Park asks $25/SF/yr plus all utilities. That is roughly $35,600 a year, or about $2,970 a month, in base rent. The College Park retail average on LoopNet is about $36/SF. This is a comparable listing, not necessarily the owner's storefront.","source":"https://www.loopnet.com/Listing/7236-7370-Baltimore-Ave-College-Park-MD/17610853/","verified":true},{"claim":"Bakery benchmarks: food cost of 28% to 35% of revenue, labor of 25% to 35% including owner pay, and net profit of 5% to 15%. Other sources put the average retail bakery at 4% to 9% net. At $42K a month, a 5% to 15% margin is roughly $2,100 to $6,300 a month before owner pay.","source":"https://www.bakeonyx.ai/resources/bakery-benchmarks","verified":true},{"claim":"The Prince George's County minimum wage is $15.30/hr for all employers, above the $15.00 state rate. College Park is in Prince George's County, so a second location in the county would pay the same rate. A location in a neighboring county such as Montgomery or Howard would face different, higher local wage rates.","source":"https://fingercheck.com/payroll-software/maryland-minimum-wage/","verified":true},{"claim":"Prince George's County requires a food service facility permit from the Health Department and plan review through the permitting agency. A certified food service manager must be on duty during all hours of operation. First-time certification is a 16-hour course plus an exam, with a $35 county fee. A second storefront would need its own permit and certified manager coverage, which matters because the owner has no manager.","source":"https://www.dcfoodhandlers.com/local-requirements/prince-georges-county-health-department.html","verified":true},{"claim":"A Prince George's County fee schedule lists food service facility permit fees of $449 (low HACCP priority), $660 (moderate) and $845 (high), each tied to a $66 administrative fee. The schedule is older and a March 2025 revision exists, so confirm current fees with the county. Operating without a certified manager carries a listed fee of about $600 or more.","source":"https://www.princegeorgescountymd.gov/sites/default/files/media-document/DPIE%20Fee%20Schedule.pdf","verified":true},{"claim":"DoorDash Marketplace charges no monthly or signup fee. Delivery commission is 15% on Basic, 25% on Plus and 30% on Premier, and pickup is 6%. Free trials are 7 days at 0% on Basic and 30 days on Plus and Premier.","source":"https://merchants.doordash.com/en-us/pricing","verified":true},{"claim":"Uber Eats Marketplace delivery commission is 20% on Lite, 25% on Plus (30% on Uber One member orders) and 30% on Premium. Lite was raised from 15% in March 2026. Pickup is 7% with validated in-store pricing.","source":"https://www.uber.com/us/en/legal/uber-eats-merchant-terms-and-conditions/","verified":true},{"claim":"Square for Restaurants (the owner's current POS) is $0/mo on Square Free, $49/mo per location on Square Plus and $149/mo per location on Square Premium. A KDS screen is $30/mo per device on Plus and $20 on Premium. Plans are per location, so a second store adds its own subscription. There are no contracts.","source":"https://squareup.com/us/en/campaigns/discover-restaurants","verified":true},{"claim":"Square's own online ordering page (pickup and local delivery) is included on every plan with no marketplace-style commission. Card processing online is 3.3% + 30¢ on Free and 2.9% + 30¢ on Plus and Premium. In-person card processing is 2.6% + 15¢ on Free, 2.5% + 15¢ on Plus and 2.4% + 15¢ on Premium.","source":"https://www.therestauranthq.com/technology/square-for-restaurants-review/","verified":true},{"claim":"Square Online delivery can use a third-party courier such as DoorDash or Uber, which charges per delivery. One report cites about $6.99 and up per delivery, and Square's own per-delivery fee was dropped in February 2025. This is a secondary source, so confirm on Square's delivery pricing page.","source":"https://www.adamssnackwagon.com/square-online-ordering-for-restaurants/","verified":true}],"open_questions":["What are the Route 1 space's square footage, rent type (NNN or gross), lease term, personal guarantee, rent-free build-out period and landlord tenant-improvement allowance? Is it a former food space with a hood, grease trap and three-phase power?","What do the first store's actual monthly profit, owner pay and cash cushion look like? $42K a month in revenue does not show what is left after the owner's own pay.","How seasonal is the first store's revenue with the University of Maryland calendar (summer, winter break)? Would a Route 1 location face the same swings?","Who runs the first store's morning bake if the owner is at the second location? What would a qualified manager or head baker cost, and is a certified food service manager already on staff?","What share of current sales might delivery or online ordering add? How much do customers already ask for delivery, and can baked goods hold up in transit?","Is the second location meant to be a full bakery or a retail satellite fed from the existing kitchen? Does the current kitchen have spare oven capacity?","Could financing (such as an SBA loan) or a landlord allowance cover build-out, so savings stay as a reserve for the first store?","What would signing the lease cost in total (deposit, personal guarantee, 5-year obligation) if the second store underperforms?","What are the College Park and Prince George's County zoning, signage and use-and-occupancy requirements for this specific storefront, and how long would approvals take?"]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/researcher@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/researcher@1"]},"id":"b5763ce8-dd3e-4e0c-9c12-0ea7b9ba5c7f","timestamp":1794351725.9841762}

data: {"invocationId":"e-472eff97-dd30-44e7-a678-50422083d0a1","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{"evidence":{"decision":"Should Rise & Shine Bakery (College Park, MD) sign a lease and open a second location, or invest in delivery first, or neither?","options":["Do nothing for now: keep one location, build cash, and revisit in 6 to 12 months","Delivery and online ordering first: use the existing Square online page plus a limited marketplace test (DoorDash Basic or Uber Eats Lite) or a small in-house delivery radius","Open the second location on Route 1 now, using savings and possibly financing","Sequence it: hire or train a manager and test delivery for 90 days, then negotiate the Route 1 lease or look at other spaces"],"facts":[{"claim":"A second retail bakery location is commonly estimated at $75K to $200K, and bakery startup costs overall at $50K to $250K. The lower end of that range is close to the owner's $90,000 in savings. Estimates vary widely by source and by space condition.","source":"https://primerates.com/business-loans/bakery-business-loans/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:53Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Another industry guide puts a retail bakery in a second-generation food space at $120K to $350K, and a raw-shell bakery-cafe at $350K to $700K or more. It says deck ovens often need three-phase power, and retrofitting a space without it can cost $10K to $40K.","source":"https://www.tableview.com/blog/how-to-open-a-bakery/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"A space that already has a hood, grease trap and three-compartment sink can cut build-out cost by about 40% to 60%. Whether the Route 1 storefront has these is unknown and should be checked before signing.","source":"https://startupcosthub.com/costs/bakery-startup-costs","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"A LoopNet listing for a 1,425 SF retail suite on Route 1 (Baltimore Ave) in College Park asks $25/SF/yr plus all utilities. That is roughly $35,600 a year, or about $2,970 a month, in base rent. The College Park retail average on LoopNet is about $36/SF. This is a comparable listing, not necessarily the owner's storefront.","source":"https://www.loopnet.com/Listing/7236-7370-Baltimore-Ave-College-Park-MD/17610853/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"unreachable","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":403}},{"claim":"Bakery benchmarks: food cost of 28% to 35% of revenue, labor of 25% to 35% including owner pay, and net profit of 5% to 15%. Other sources put the average retail bakery at 4% to 9% net. At $42K a month, a 5% to 15% margin is roughly $2,100 to $6,300 a month before owner pay.","source":"https://www.bakeonyx.ai/resources/bakery-benchmarks","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"The Prince George's County minimum wage is $15.30/hr for all employers, above the $15.00 state rate. College Park is in Prince George's County, so a second location in the county would pay the same rate. A location in a neighboring county such as Montgomery or Howard would face different, higher local wage rates.","source":"https://fingercheck.com/payroll-software/maryland-minimum-wage/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Prince George's County requires a food service facility permit from the Health Department and plan review through the permitting agency. A certified food service manager must be on duty during all hours of operation. First-time certification is a 16-hour course plus an exam, with a $35 county fee. A second storefront would need its own permit and certified manager coverage, which matters because the owner has no manager.","source":"https://www.dcfoodhandlers.com/local-requirements/prince-georges-county-health-department.html","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:53Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"A Prince George's County fee schedule lists food service facility permit fees of $449 (low HACCP priority), $660 (moderate) and $845 (high), each tied to a $66 administrative fee. The schedule is older and a March 2025 revision exists, so confirm current fees with the county. Operating without a certified manager carries a listed fee of about $600 or more.","source":"https://www.princegeorgescountymd.gov/sites/default/files/media-document/DPIE%20Fee%20Schedule.pdf","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"DoorDash Marketplace charges no monthly or signup fee. Delivery commission is 15% on Basic, 25% on Plus and 30% on Premier, and pickup is 6%. Free trials are 7 days at 0% on Basic and 30 days on Plus and Premier.","source":"https://merchants.doordash.com/en-us/pricing","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Uber Eats Marketplace delivery commission is 20% on Lite, 25% on Plus (30% on Uber One member orders) and 30% on Premium. Lite was raised from 15% in March 2026. Pickup is 7% with validated in-store pricing.","source":"https://www.uber.com/us/en/legal/uber-eats-merchant-terms-and-conditions/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Square for Restaurants (the owner's current POS) is $0/mo on Square Free, $49/mo per location on Square Plus and $149/mo per location on Square Premium. A KDS screen is $30/mo per device on Plus and $20 on Premium. Plans are per location, so a second store adds its own subscription. There are no contracts.","source":"https://squareup.com/us/en/campaigns/discover-restaurants","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:53Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Square's own online ordering page (pickup and local delivery) is included on every plan with no marketplace-style commission. Card processing online is 3.3% + 30¢ on Free and 2.9% + 30¢ on Plus and Premium. In-person card processing is 2.6% + 15¢ on Free, 2.5% + 15¢ on Plus and 2.4% + 15¢ on Premium.","source":"https://www.therestauranthq.com/technology/square-for-restaurants-review/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Square Online delivery can use a third-party courier such as DoorDash or Uber, which charges per delivery. One report cites about $6.99 and up per delivery, and Square's own per-delivery fee was dropped in February 2025. This is a secondary source, so confirm on Square's delivery pricing page.","source":"https://www.adamssnackwagon.com/square-online-ordering-for-restaurants/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}}],"open_questions":["What are the Route 1 space's square footage, rent type (NNN or gross), lease term, personal guarantee, rent-free build-out period and landlord tenant-improvement allowance? Is it a former food space with a hood, grease trap and three-phase power?","What do the first store's actual monthly profit, owner pay and cash cushion look like? $42K a month in revenue does not show what is left after the owner's own pay.","How seasonal is the first store's revenue with the University of Maryland calendar (summer, winter break)? Would a Route 1 location face the same swings?","Who runs the first store's morning bake if the owner is at the second location? What would a qualified manager or head baker cost, and is a certified food service manager already on staff?","What share of current sales might delivery or online ordering add? How much do customers already ask for delivery, and can baked goods hold up in transit?","Is the second location meant to be a full bakery or a retail satellite fed from the existing kitchen? Does the current kitchen have spare oven capacity?","Could financing (such as an SBA loan) or a landlord allowance cover build-out, so savings stay as a reserve for the first store?","What would signing the lease cost in total (deposit, personal guarantee, 5-year obligation) if the second store underperforms?","What are the College Park and Prince George's County zoning, signage and use-and-occupancy requirements for this specific storefront, and how long would approvals take?"]}},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"decision":"Should Rise & Shine Bakery (College Park, MD) sign a lease and open a second location, or invest in delivery first, or neither?","options":["Do nothing for now: keep one location, build cash, and revisit in 6 to 12 months","Delivery and online ordering first: use the existing Square online page plus a limited marketplace test (DoorDash Basic or Uber Eats Lite) or a small in-house delivery radius","Open the second location on Route 1 now, using savings and possibly financing","Sequence it: hire or train a manager and test delivery for 90 days, then negotiate the Route 1 lease or look at other spaces"],"facts":[{"claim":"A second retail bakery location is commonly estimated at $75K to $200K, and bakery startup costs overall at $50K to $250K. The lower end of that range is close to the owner's $90,000 in savings. Estimates vary widely by source and by space condition.","source":"https://primerates.com/business-loans/bakery-business-loans/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:53Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Another industry guide puts a retail bakery in a second-generation food space at $120K to $350K, and a raw-shell bakery-cafe at $350K to $700K or more. It says deck ovens often need three-phase power, and retrofitting a space without it can cost $10K to $40K.","source":"https://www.tableview.com/blog/how-to-open-a-bakery/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"A space that already has a hood, grease trap and three-compartment sink can cut build-out cost by about 40% to 60%. Whether the Route 1 storefront has these is unknown and should be checked before signing.","source":"https://startupcosthub.com/costs/bakery-startup-costs","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"A LoopNet listing for a 1,425 SF retail suite on Route 1 (Baltimore Ave) in College Park asks $25/SF/yr plus all utilities. That is roughly $35,600 a year, or about $2,970 a month, in base rent. The College Park retail average on LoopNet is about $36/SF. This is a comparable listing, not necessarily the owner's storefront.","source":"https://www.loopnet.com/Listing/7236-7370-Baltimore-Ave-College-Park-MD/17610853/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"unreachable","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":403}},{"claim":"Bakery benchmarks: food cost of 28% to 35% of revenue, labor of 25% to 35% including owner pay, and net profit of 5% to 15%. Other sources put the average retail bakery at 4% to 9% net. At $42K a month, a 5% to 15% margin is roughly $2,100 to $6,300 a month before owner pay.","source":"https://www.bakeonyx.ai/resources/bakery-benchmarks","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"The Prince George's County minimum wage is $15.30/hr for all employers, above the $15.00 state rate. College Park is in Prince George's County, so a second location in the county would pay the same rate. A location in a neighboring county such as Montgomery or Howard would face different, higher local wage rates.","source":"https://fingercheck.com/payroll-software/maryland-minimum-wage/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Prince George's County requires a food service facility permit from the Health Department and plan review through the permitting agency. A certified food service manager must be on duty during all hours of operation. First-time certification is a 16-hour course plus an exam, with a $35 county fee. A second storefront would need its own permit and certified manager coverage, which matters because the owner has no manager.","source":"https://www.dcfoodhandlers.com/local-requirements/prince-georges-county-health-department.html","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:53Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"A Prince George's County fee schedule lists food service facility permit fees of $449 (low HACCP priority), $660 (moderate) and $845 (high), each tied to a $66 administrative fee. The schedule is older and a March 2025 revision exists, so confirm current fees with the county. Operating without a certified manager carries a listed fee of about $600 or more.","source":"https://www.princegeorgescountymd.gov/sites/default/files/media-document/DPIE%20Fee%20Schedule.pdf","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"DoorDash Marketplace charges no monthly or signup fee. Delivery commission is 15% on Basic, 25% on Plus and 30% on Premier, and pickup is 6%. Free trials are 7 days at 0% on Basic and 30 days on Plus and Premier.","source":"https://merchants.doordash.com/en-us/pricing","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Uber Eats Marketplace delivery commission is 20% on Lite, 25% on Plus (30% on Uber One member orders) and 30% on Premium. Lite was raised from 15% in March 2026. Pickup is 7% with validated in-store pricing.","source":"https://www.uber.com/us/en/legal/uber-eats-merchant-terms-and-conditions/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Square for Restaurants (the owner's current POS) is $0/mo on Square Free, $49/mo per location on Square Plus and $149/mo per location on Square Premium. A KDS screen is $30/mo per device on Plus and $20 on Premium. Plans are per location, so a second store adds its own subscription. There are no contracts.","source":"https://squareup.com/us/en/campaigns/discover-restaurants","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:53Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Square's own online ordering page (pickup and local delivery) is included on every plan with no marketplace-style commission. Card processing online is 3.3% + 30¢ on Free and 2.9% + 30¢ on Plus and Premium. In-person card processing is 2.6% + 15¢ on Free, 2.5% + 15¢ on Plus and 2.4% + 15¢ on Premium.","source":"https://www.therestauranthq.com/technology/square-for-restaurants-review/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}},{"claim":"Square Online delivery can use a third-party courier such as DoorDash or Uber, which charges per delivery. One report cites about $6.99 and up per delivery, and Square's own per-delivery fee was dropped in February 2025. This is a secondary source, so confirm on Square's delivery pricing page.","source":"https://www.adamssnackwagon.com/square-online-ordering-for-restaurants/","verified":true,"check":{"status":"ok","checked_at":"2026-11-10T23:02:52Z","http_status":200}}],"open_questions":["What are the Route 1 space's square footage, rent type (NNN or gross), lease term, personal guarantee, rent-free build-out period and landlord tenant-improvement allowance? Is it a former food space with a hood, grease trap and three-phase power?","What do the first store's actual monthly profit, owner pay and cash cushion look like? $42K a month in revenue does not show what is left after the owner's own pay.","How seasonal is the first store's revenue with the University of Maryland calendar (summer, winter break)? Would a Route 1 location face the same swings?","Who runs the first store's morning bake if the owner is at the second location? What would a qualified manager or head baker cost, and is a certified food service manager already on staff?","What share of current sales might delivery or online ordering add? How much do customers already ask for delivery, and can baked goods hold up in transit?","Is the second location meant to be a full bakery or a retail satellite fed from the existing kitchen? Does the current kitchen have spare oven capacity?","Could financing (such as an SBA loan) or a landlord allowance cover build-out, so savings stay as a reserve for the first store?","What would signing the lease cost in total (deposit, personal guarantee, 5-year obligation) if the second store underperforms?","What are the College Park and Prince George's County zoning, signage and use-and-occupancy requirements for this specific storefront, and how long would approvals take?"]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/check_sources@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/check_sources@1"]},"id":"0a673270-fa87-4a58-9624-58a3467ee25d","timestamp":1794351773.7635603}

data: {"invocationId":"e-472eff97-dd30-44e7-a678-50422083d0a1","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":"ROUND 1 — give your independent opinion. You have not seen the other seats yet.","nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/convene@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/convene@1"]},"id":"5e78c1c8-ba8f-413f-88c5-16791bc643f4","timestamp":1794351773.7669165}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Delay the Route 1 lease; test delivery and a manager for 90 days first.\",\"summary\":\"Signing now would commit most of your $90K savings and a multi-year lease before you know if the first store can run without you. Your own morning bake is the bottleneck, and you have no manager. Delivery is cheap to test and can show demand. Sign only if the space needs little build-out, the lease has an allowance, and a manager is in place.\",\"key_points\":[\"Build-out for a second bakery runs $75K to $200K (F1), close to your entire savings, which leaves no reserve for the first store (F1, F6).\",\"You bake every morning and have no manager; a second store also needs its own certified food service manager on duty at all hours (F7).\",\"Delivery test costs little: Square online ordering has no commission (F12), while marketplaces take 15% to 30% (F9, F10).\"],\"risks\":[\"Signing a 5-year lease with a personal guarantee could leave you liable if the second store underperforms (open question on lease terms).\",\"Without a manager, a split between two sites could hurt quality at the first store and drive away regulars.\",\"Waiting may lose the Route 1 space, though a sequenced plan can still negotiate or find other space later.\"],\"confidence\":70,\"pushback\":\"You assume the Route 1 space is worth taking at all. Ask whether it has a hood, grease trap, and three-phase power (F2, F3), and what the total five-year cost is, before you treat the lease as a growth opportunity.\\n\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":498,"promptTokenCount":3796,"totalTokenCount":4294},"invocationId":"e-472eff97-dd30-44e7-a678-50422083d0a1","author":"strategist_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Delay the Route 1 lease; test delivery and a manager for 90 days first.","summary":"Signing now would commit most of your $90K savings and a multi-year lease before you know if the first store can run without you. Your own morning bake is the bottleneck, and you have no manager. Delivery is cheap to test and can show demand. Sign only if the space needs little build-out, the lease has an allowance, and a manager is in place.","key_points":["Build-out for a second bakery runs $75K to $200K (F1), close to your entire savings, which leaves no reserve for the first store (F1, F6).","You bake every morning and have no manager; a second store also needs its own certified food service manager on duty at all hours (F7).","Delivery test costs little: Square online ordering has no commission (F12), while marketplaces take 15% to 30% (F9, F10)."],"risks":["Signing a 5-year lease with a personal guarantee could leave you liable if the second store underperforms (open question on lease terms).","Without a manager, a split between two sites could hurt quality at the first store and drive away regulars.","Waiting may lose the Route 1 space, though a sequenced plan can still negotiate or find other space later."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"You assume the Route 1 space is worth taking at all. Ask whether it has a hood, grease trap, and three-phase power (F2, F3), and what the total five-year cost is, before you treat the lease as a growth opportunity.\n"},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/strategist_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/strategist_r1@1"]},"branch":"strategist_r1@1","id":"b0c11d55-2a04-4ec8-9955-bc7ed420a6e1","timestamp":1794351773.779601}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"oppose\",\"headline\":\"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet; test delivery and a manager first.\",\"summary\":\"Opening a second store now would put most of your $90,000 cushion at risk. Your first store's real profit after your own pay is unknown. No manager exists, and you bake every morning. Test delivery and hire a manager for 90 days first, then decide with real numbers.\",\"key_points\":[\"Build-out for a second bakery is commonly $75K to $200K (F1), so your savings could be almost fully used before the first sales (F1, F3).\",\"At $42K a month, a 5% to 15% margin is about $2,100 to $6,300 before owner pay (F5), and no one has shown what is left after your pay.\",\"A second store needs its own permit and a certified manager on duty at all hours (F7), and you have no manager (brief).\"],\"risks\":[\"Build-out overruns, such as three-phase power retrofits costing $10K to $40K (F2), drain savings and leave no runway for the first store.\",\"A five-year lease obligation (open question) could outlast a weak second store, with a personal guarantee possibly exposing your home (confirm terms with an attorney).\",\"Your morning bake has no backup; if you split time across two sites, quality or the first store's sales could drop.\"],\"confidence\":72,\"pushback\":\"You assume the Route 1 space will be cheap and quick to open. Its hood, grease trap, power and rent type are unknown (F3, open questions). Ask whether you would still sign if build-out costs ran $150K with a 5-year lease.\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":509,"promptTokenCount":3849,"totalTokenCount":4358},"invocationId":"e-472eff97-dd30-44e7-a678-50422083d0a1","author":"devils_advocate_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet; test delivery and a manager first.","summary":"Opening a second store now would put most of your $90,000 cushion at risk. Your first store's real profit after your own pay is unknown. No manager exists, and you bake every morning. Test delivery and hire a manager for 90 days first, then decide with real numbers.","key_points":["Build-out for a second bakery is commonly $75K to $200K (F1), so your savings could be almost fully used before the first sales (F1, F3).","At $42K a month, a 5% to 15% margin is about $2,100 to $6,300 before owner pay (F5), and no one has shown what is left after your pay.","A second store needs its own permit and a certified manager on duty at all hours (F7), and you have no manager (brief)."],"risks":["Build-out overruns, such as three-phase power retrofits costing $10K to $40K (F2), drain savings and leave no runway for the first store.","A five-year lease obligation (open question) could outlast a weak second store, with a personal guarantee possibly exposing your home (confirm terms with an attorney).","Your morning bake has no backup; if you split time across two sites, quality or the first store's sales could drop."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"You assume the Route 1 space will be cheap and quick to open. Its hood, grease trap, power and rent type are unknown (F3, open questions). Ask whether you would still sign if build-out costs ran $150K with a 5-year lease."},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/devils_advocate_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/devils_advocate_r1@1"]},"branch":"devils_advocate_r1@1","id":"822a5491-0ce7-461e-9a28-d959d2d15475","timestamp":1794351773.7804372}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"oppose\",\"headline\":\"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet; test delivery and a manager first.\",\"summary\":\"Opening now would put most of your $90,000 savings into build-out and rent before you know the first store's real profit. Rent alone is about $2,970 a month for a comparable suite (F4), and a bakery build-out can run $75K to $200K (F1). Your first store also has no manager while you bake every morning. Test delivery for 90 days, hire or train a manager, and revisit the lease with facts in hand.\",\"key_points\":[\"Build-out cost could consume nearly all savings: $75K to $200K for a second bakery (F1), with $10K to $40K more if three-phase power is missing (F2).\",\"Your margin is thin: bakery net profit runs 5% to 15%, roughly $2,100 to $6,300 a month before owner pay (F5). A second rent bill of about $2,970 a month eats much of that.\",\"Delivery through Square online avoids marketplace commissions of 15% to 30% (F9, F10), and Square's online ordering is included on every plan (F12).\"],\"risks\":[\"You bake every morning, so a second store would leave the first with no head baker or certified manager, which is required on duty during all hours (F7).\",\"If the second store underperforms, the lease is a multi-year obligation that could put the first store at risk.\",\"Estimates vary widely and the Route 1 space's condition is unknown; a space without a hood, grease trap or three-phase power could cost far more than planned (F2, F3).\"],\"confidence\":78,\"pushback\":\"You assume the second location will add profit rather than split your attention and cash. Ask what your first store nets after your own pay, and whether it can run well without you in the morning.(unverified)\"}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":570,"promptTokenCount":3800,"totalTokenCount":4370},"invocationId":"e-472eff97-dd30-44e7-a678-50422083d0a1","author":"industry_expert_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet; test delivery and a manager first.","summary":"Opening now would put most of your $90,000 savings into build-out and rent before you know the first store's real profit. Rent alone is about $2,970 a month for a comparable suite (F4), and a bakery build-out can run $75K to $200K (F1). Your first store also has no manager while you bake every morning. Test delivery for 90 days, hire or train a manager, and revisit the lease with facts in hand.","key_points":["Build-out cost could consume nearly all savings: $75K to $200K for a second bakery (F1), with $10K to $40K more if three-phase power is missing (F2).","Your margin is thin: bakery net profit runs 5% to 15%, roughly $2,100 to $6,300 a month before owner pay (F5). A second rent bill of about $2,970 a month eats much of that.","Delivery through Square online avoids marketplace commissions of 15% to 30% (F9, F10), and Square's online ordering is included on every plan (F12)."],"risks":["You bake every morning, so a second store would leave the first with no head baker or certified manager, which is required on duty during all hours (F7).","If the second store underperforms, the lease is a multi-year obligation that could put the first store at risk.","Estimates vary widely and the Route 1 space's condition is unknown; a space without a hood, grease trap or three-phase power could cost far more than planned (F2, F3)."],"confidence":78,"pushback":"You assume the second location will add profit rather than split your attention and cash. Ask what your first store nets after your own pay, and whether it can run well without you in the morning.(unverified)"},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/industry_expert_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/industry_expert_r1@1"]},"branch":"industry_expert_r1@1","id":"00ebc8c9-20eb-4ae0-80be-248178cdc52b","timestamp":1794351773.774773}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"conditional\",\"headline\":\"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet; sequence manager and delivery first.\",\"summary\":\"Opening a second store now would use most of your $90,000 savings, which is the cushion protecting your first store. Build-out may cost $75K to $200K (F1), and the space may need costly fixes (F2). You also have no manager, and a second store needs its own permit and certified manager coverage (F7). Test delivery and hire or train a manager for 90 days first, then negotiate the lease with real numbers.\",\"key_points\":[\"Build-out range (F1, F2) could consume most or all of $90,000 before rent and payroll.\",\"No manager and a required certified food service manager on every shift make a second store unsafe to staff now (F7).\",\"Delivery can be tested cheaply via Square online ordering with no commission (F12), with marketplace fees of 15% to 30% as a comparison (F9, F10).\"],\"risks\":[\"Build-out overruns or missing hood, grease trap or three-phase power could push costs toward the high end (F2, F3 unknown).\",\"Base rent near $2,970 a month plus utilities on a 5-year lease, if the store underperforms (F4, unverified for this space).\",\"Owner stretched between two kitchens, weakening the first store that currently covers your runway.\"],\"confidence\":72,\"pushback\":\"Assumes the first store's profit is strong enough to fund expansion; the briefing does not show profit after owner pay, so verify that before any lease.\",\"checks\":[{\"area\":\"financial\",\"status\":\"block\",\"note\":\"Build-out ($75K to $200K, F1) against $90K savings leaves little or no reserve for the first store; confirm with an accountant and the local Small Business Development Center.\"},{\"area\":\"compliance\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Second store needs its own Prince George's County food service facility permit and certified manager coverage (F7, F8); fees should be confirmed with the county, and the owner has no manager.\"},{\"area\":\"legal\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Lease terms (personal guarantee, deposit, 5-year obligation, NNN costs) are unknown; have an attorney review before signing (unverified for this space).\"},{\"area\":\"operational\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Space may lack hood, grease trap or three-phase power; retrofit could cost $10K to $40K (F2, F3); check before signing.\"},{\"area\":\"goals\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"note\":\"Delivery via Square online ordering serves the revenue goal at low cost and low risk (F12), and protects the first store as the goal requires.\"},{\"area\":\"compliance\",\"status\":\"flag\",\"note\":\"Prince George's County minimum wage is $15.30/hr (F6); a second store in a neighboring county would face different wage rates (unverified).\"}]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":902,"promptTokenCount":4186,"totalTokenCount":5088},"invocationId":"e-472eff97-dd30-44e7-a678-50422083d0a1","author":"realist_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet; sequence manager and delivery first.","summary":"Opening a second store now would use most of your $90,000 savings, which is the cushion protecting your first store. Build-out may cost $75K to $200K (F1), and the space may need costly fixes (F2). You also have no manager, and a second store needs its own permit and certified manager coverage (F7). Test delivery and hire or train a manager for 90 days first, then negotiate the lease with real numbers.","key_points":["Build-out range (F1, F2) could consume most or all of $90,000 before rent and payroll.","No manager and a required certified food service manager on every shift make a second store unsafe to staff now (F7).","Delivery can be tested cheaply via Square online ordering with no commission (F12), with marketplace fees of 15% to 30% as a comparison (F9, F10)."],"risks":["Build-out overruns or missing hood, grease trap or three-phase power could push costs toward the high end (F2, F3 unknown).","Base rent near $2,970 a month plus utilities on a 5-year lease, if the store underperforms (F4, unverified for this space).","Owner stretched between two kitchens, weakening the first store that currently covers your runway."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumes the first store's profit is strong enough to fund expansion; the briefing does not show profit after owner pay, so verify that before any lease.","checks":[{"area":"financial","status":"block","note":"Build-out ($75K to $200K, F1) against $90K savings leaves little or no reserve for the first store; confirm with an accountant and the local Small Business Development Center."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"Second store needs its own Prince George's County food service facility permit and certified manager coverage (F7, F8); fees should be confirmed with the county, and the owner has no manager."},{"area":"legal","status":"flag","note":"Lease terms (personal guarantee, deposit, 5-year obligation, NNN costs) are unknown; have an attorney review before signing (unverified for this space)."},{"area":"operational","status":"flag","note":"Space may lack hood, grease trap or three-phase power; retrofit could cost $10K to $40K (F2, F3); check before signing."},{"area":"goals","status":"pass","note":"Delivery via Square online ordering serves the revenue goal at low cost and low risk (F12), and protects the first store as the goal requires."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"Prince George's County minimum wage is $15.30/hr (F6); a second store in a neighboring county would face different wage rates (unverified)."}]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/realist_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/realist_r1@1"]},"branch":"realist_r1@1","id":"55a82c80-edc4-4921-8b7e-3c0016c2fa4d","timestamp":1794351773.7785323}

data: {"modelVersion":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","content":{"parts":[{"text":"{\"stance\":\"oppose\",\"headline\":\"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet; test delivery and a manager first.\",\"summary\":\"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet. A build-out could cost $75K to $200K, most of your $90,000 savings. You also have no manager to run the first store while you work a new one. Test delivery first, train or hire a manager, and negotiate the lease only after the first store's cash cushion is proven.\",\"key_points\":[\"Build-out could run $75K to $200K (F1), most of your $90K savings, leaving little cushion for the first store.\",\"A comparable Route 1 suite costs about $2,970 a month in base rent plus utilities (F4), before staff and food costs of 28% to 35% of revenue (F5).\",\"Prince George's County requires a certified food service manager on duty during all hours (F7), and you have no manager yet.\"],\"risks\":[\"The Route 1 space may lack a hood, grease trap or three-phase power; a retrofit can add $10K to $40K (F2, F3).\",\"Your morning bake is the bottleneck. If you split time between two stores, quality or output at the first store may slip.\",\"Lease terms such as a personal guarantee and a multi-year obligation could stay with you if the second store underperforms (terms unverified).\"],\"confidence\":75,\"pushback\":\"Assumes the Route 1 storefront is a good deal. You have not yet checked its build-out condition, lease terms or whether it has a hood and grease trap.\",\"tools\":[{\"name\":\"DoorDash Marketplace (Basic plan)\",\"use\":\"Test real delivery demand for your baked goods before committing to a second lease.\",\"monthly_cost\":\"$0/mo plus 15% commission per delivery order\",\"works_with\":\"Standalone listing; alongside your Square POS (integration unverified)\",\"caveat\":\"The 15% commission can erase margin on small orders, and baked goods may not travel well.\",\"url\":\"https://merchants.doordash.com/en-us/pricing\",\"price_fact\":\"F9\",\"pilot_test\":\"Keep it if delivery orders are profitable after commission and food cost over 60 days; otherwise cancel at day 60.\"}]}"}],"role":"model"},"partial":false,"finishReason":"STOP","usageMetadata":{"cachedContentTokenCount":0,"candidatesTokenCount":1870,"promptTokenCount":4823,"thoughtsTokenCount":1166,"totalTokenCount":6693},"invocationId":"e-472eff97-dd30-44e7-a678-50422083d0a1","author":"ai_tools_expert_r1","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet; test delivery and a manager first.","summary":"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet. A build-out could cost $75K to $200K, most of your $90,000 savings. You also have no manager to run the first store while you work a new one. Test delivery first, train or hire a manager, and negotiate the lease only after the first store's cash cushion is proven.","key_points":["Build-out could run $75K to $200K (F1), most of your $90K savings, leaving little cushion for the first store.","A comparable Route 1 suite costs about $2,970 a month in base rent plus utilities (F4), before staff and food costs of 28% to 35% of revenue (F5).","Prince George's County requires a certified food service manager on duty during all hours (F7), and you have no manager yet."],"risks":["The Route 1 space may lack a hood, grease trap or three-phase power; a retrofit can add $10K to $40K (F2, F3).","Your morning bake is the bottleneck. If you split time between two stores, quality or output at the first store may slip.","Lease terms such as a personal guarantee and a multi-year obligation could stay with you if the second store underperforms (terms unverified)."],"confidence":75,"pushback":"Assumes the Route 1 storefront is a good deal. You have not yet checked its build-out condition, lease terms or whether it has a hood and grease trap.","tools":[{"name":"DoorDash Marketplace (Basic plan)","use":"Test real delivery demand for your baked goods before committing to a second lease.","monthly_cost":"$0/mo plus 15% commission per delivery order","works_with":"Standalone listing; alongside your Square POS (integration unverified)","caveat":"The 15% commission can erase margin on small orders, and baked goods may not travel well.","url":"https://merchants.doordash.com/en-us/pricing","price_fact":"F9","pilot_test":"Keep it if delivery orders are profitable after commission and food cost over 60 days; otherwise cancel at day 60."}]},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/ai_tools_expert_r1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/ai_tools_expert_r1@1"]},"branch":"ai_tools_expert_r1@1","id":"25fa11c4-4d58-4cce-a61e-fdf0b6437007","timestamp":1794351773.7760034}

data: {"invocationId":"e-472eff97-dd30-44e7-a678-50422083d0a1","author":"crucible","actions":{"stateDelta":{},"artifactDelta":{},"requestedAuthConfigs":{},"requestedToolConfirmations":{}},"output":{"industry_expert_r1":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet; test delivery and a manager first.","summary":"Opening now would put most of your $90,000 savings into build-out and rent before you know the first store's real profit. Rent alone is about $2,970 a month for a comparable suite (F4), and a bakery build-out can run $75K to $200K (F1). Your first store also has no manager while you bake every morning. Test delivery for 90 days, hire or train a manager, and revisit the lease with facts in hand.","key_points":["Build-out cost could consume nearly all savings: $75K to $200K for a second bakery (F1), with $10K to $40K more if three-phase power is missing (F2).","Your margin is thin: bakery net profit runs 5% to 15%, roughly $2,100 to $6,300 a month before owner pay (F5). A second rent bill of about $2,970 a month eats much of that.","Delivery through Square online avoids marketplace commissions of 15% to 30% (F9, F10), and Square's online ordering is included on every plan (F12)."],"risks":["You bake every morning, so a second store would leave the first with no head baker or certified manager, which is required on duty during all hours (F7).","If the second store underperforms, the lease is a multi-year obligation that could put the first store at risk.","Estimates vary widely and the Route 1 space's condition is unknown; a space without a hood, grease trap or three-phase power could cost far more than planned (F2, F3)."],"confidence":78,"pushback":"You assume the second location will add profit rather than split your attention and cash. Ask what your first store nets after your own pay, and whether it can run well without you in the morning.(unverified)"},"ai_tools_expert_r1":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet; test delivery and a manager first.","summary":"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet. A build-out could cost $75K to $200K, most of your $90,000 savings. You also have no manager to run the first store while you work a new one. Test delivery first, train or hire a manager, and negotiate the lease only after the first store's cash cushion is proven.","key_points":["Build-out could run $75K to $200K (F1), most of your $90K savings, leaving little cushion for the first store.","A comparable Route 1 suite costs about $2,970 a month in base rent plus utilities (F4), before staff and food costs of 28% to 35% of revenue (F5).","Prince George's County requires a certified food service manager on duty during all hours (F7), and you have no manager yet."],"risks":["The Route 1 space may lack a hood, grease trap or three-phase power; a retrofit can add $10K to $40K (F2, F3).","Your morning bake is the bottleneck. If you split time between two stores, quality or output at the first store may slip.","Lease terms such as a personal guarantee and a multi-year obligation could stay with you if the second store underperforms (terms unverified)."],"confidence":75,"pushback":"Assumes the Route 1 storefront is a good deal. You have not yet checked its build-out condition, lease terms or whether it has a hood and grease trap.","tools":[{"name":"DoorDash Marketplace (Basic plan)","use":"Test real delivery demand for your baked goods before committing to a second lease.","monthly_cost":"$0/mo plus 15% commission per delivery order","works_with":"Standalone listing; alongside your Square POS (integration unverified)","caveat":"The 15% commission can erase margin on small orders, and baked goods may not travel well.","url":"https://merchants.doordash.com/en-us/pricing","price_fact":"F9","pilot_test":"Keep it if delivery orders are profitable after commission and food cost over 60 days; otherwise cancel at day 60."}]},"realist_r1":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet; sequence manager and delivery first.","summary":"Opening a second store now would use most of your $90,000 savings, which is the cushion protecting your first store. Build-out may cost $75K to $200K (F1), and the space may need costly fixes (F2). You also have no manager, and a second store needs its own permit and certified manager coverage (F7). Test delivery and hire or train a manager for 90 days first, then negotiate the lease with real numbers.","key_points":["Build-out range (F1, F2) could consume most or all of $90,000 before rent and payroll.","No manager and a required certified food service manager on every shift make a second store unsafe to staff now (F7).","Delivery can be tested cheaply via Square online ordering with no commission (F12), with marketplace fees of 15% to 30% as a comparison (F9, F10)."],"risks":["Build-out overruns or missing hood, grease trap or three-phase power could push costs toward the high end (F2, F3 unknown).","Base rent near $2,970 a month plus utilities on a 5-year lease, if the store underperforms (F4, unverified for this space).","Owner stretched between two kitchens, weakening the first store that currently covers your runway."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"Assumes the first store's profit is strong enough to fund expansion; the briefing does not show profit after owner pay, so verify that before any lease.","checks":[{"area":"financial","status":"block","note":"Build-out ($75K to $200K, F1) against $90K savings leaves little or no reserve for the first store; confirm with an accountant and the local Small Business Development Center."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"Second store needs its own Prince George's County food service facility permit and certified manager coverage (F7, F8); fees should be confirmed with the county, and the owner has no manager."},{"area":"legal","status":"flag","note":"Lease terms (personal guarantee, deposit, 5-year obligation, NNN costs) are unknown; have an attorney review before signing (unverified for this space)."},{"area":"operational","status":"flag","note":"Space may lack hood, grease trap or three-phase power; retrofit could cost $10K to $40K (F2, F3); check before signing."},{"area":"goals","status":"pass","note":"Delivery via Square online ordering serves the revenue goal at low cost and low risk (F12), and protects the first store as the goal requires."},{"area":"compliance","status":"flag","note":"Prince George's County minimum wage is $15.30/hr (F6); a second store in a neighboring county would face different wage rates (unverified)."}]},"strategist_r1":{"stance":"conditional","headline":"Delay the Route 1 lease; test delivery and a manager for 90 days first.","summary":"Signing now would commit most of your $90K savings and a multi-year lease before you know if the first store can run without you. Your own morning bake is the bottleneck, and you have no manager. Delivery is cheap to test and can show demand. Sign only if the space needs little build-out, the lease has an allowance, and a manager is in place.","key_points":["Build-out for a second bakery runs $75K to $200K (F1), close to your entire savings, which leaves no reserve for the first store (F1, F6).","You bake every morning and have no manager; a second store also needs its own certified food service manager on duty at all hours (F7).","Delivery test costs little: Square online ordering has no commission (F12), while marketplaces take 15% to 30% (F9, F10)."],"risks":["Signing a 5-year lease with a personal guarantee could leave you liable if the second store underperforms (open question on lease terms).","Without a manager, a split between two sites could hurt quality at the first store and drive away regulars.","Waiting may lose the Route 1 space, though a sequenced plan can still negotiate or find other space later."],"confidence":70,"pushback":"You assume the Route 1 space is worth taking at all. Ask whether it has a hood, grease trap, and three-phase power (F2, F3), and what the total five-year cost is, before you treat the lease as a growth opportunity.\n"},"devils_advocate_r1":{"stance":"oppose","headline":"Don't sign the Route 1 lease yet; test delivery and a manager first.","summary":"Opening a second store now would put most of your $90,000 cushion at risk. Your first store's real profit after your own pay is unknown. No manager exists, and you bake every morning. Test delivery and hire a manager for 90 days first, then decide with real numbers.","key_points":["Build-out for a second bakery is commonly $75K to $200K (F1), so your savings could be almost fully used before the first sales (F1, F3).","At $42K a month, a 5% to 15% margin is about $2,100 to $6,300 before owner pay (F5), and no one has shown what is left after your pay.","A second store needs its own permit and a certified manager on duty at all hours (F7), and you have no manager (brief)."],"risks":["Build-out overruns, such as three-phase power retrofits costing $10K to $40K (F2), drain savings and leave no runway for the first store.","A five-year lease obligation (open question) could outlast a weak second store, with a personal guarantee possibly exposing your home (confirm terms with an attorney).","Your morning bake has no backup; if you split time across two sites, quality or the first store's sales could drop."],"confidence":72,"pushback":"You assume the Route 1 space will be cheap and quick to open. Its hood, grease trap, power and rent type are unknown (F3, open questions). Ask whether you would still sign if build-out costs ran $150K with a 5-year lease."}},"nodeInfo":{"path":"crucible@1/round1@1","outputFor":["crucible@1/round1@1"]},"id":"fb21b4e6-0e81-40be-a4db-c2344ae22d4d","timestamp":1794351783.8354468}

